%I A175769 #26 Nov 09 2026 15:51:45
%S A175769 3,6,8,11,17,28,30,45,46,56,67,79,92,106,121,137,154,172,191,211,232,
%T A175769 276
%N A175769 Maximum cardinality of isosceles sets in E^n.
%C A175769 An isosceles set is a finite set of points in Euclidean space such that among any three distinct points, at least two of the three pairwise distances are equal. - _Eric W. Weisstein_, Nov 09 2026
%C A175769 In his 1946 problem Erdős notes that a(2) = 6 and asks for the value of a(3); L. M. Kelly proves that a(2) = 6 and shows that a(3) >= 8. The editors note that Kelly disproves a conjecture of Coxeter. - _Charles R Greathouse IV_, May 21 2021
%C A175769 278 <= a(23) <= 299 (Yi, Theorem 1.3). - _Eric W. Weisstein_, Nov 09 2026
%H A175769 Paul Erdős, <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/2305860">Problems for Solution: E735</a>, Amer. Math. Monthly 53(7) (August 1946), p. 394.
%H A175769 Paul Erdős and Leroy M. Kelly, <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/2304710">Isosceles n-points</a>, Amer. Math. Monthly 54(4) (April 1947), 227-229.
%H A175769 Yury J. Ionin, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/230">Isosceles Sets</a>, Electron. J. Combin. 16(1) (2009), Art. R141.
%H A175769 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/IsoscelesSet.html">Isosceles Set</a>.
%H A175769 Hyunjun Yi, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05421">Isosceles sets and two-distance sets in Euclidean space</a>, arXiv:2611.05421 [math.MG], 2026. See Theorems 1.1 and 1.3.
%F A175769 a(n) = binomial(n+1,2) + 1 for 9 <= n <= 21, and a(22) = 276 (Yi, Theorem 1.1). - _Eric W. Weisstein_, Nov 09 2026
%Y A175769 Cf. A027627.
%K A175769 nonn,more,hard,changed
%O A175769 1,1
%A A175769 _John W. Layman_, Sep 01 2010
%E A175769 a(9)-a(22) from _Eric W. Weisstein_, Nov 09 2026
